WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.100
Weight initialization.

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:06.000
Random starting weights decide whether a network can learn at all.

3
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.767
Every layer multiplies the signal's variance by some factor.

4
00:00:11.067 --> 00:00:15.387
If the factor is below one, signals shrink toward zero across layers,

5
00:00:15.387 --> 00:00:17.767
and early layers never get a gradient.

6
00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:22.767
Above one, activations blow up layer after layer and gradients explode.

7
00:00:22.767 --> 00:00:27.600
With tanh or sigmoid they saturate instead, which makes gradients vanish.

8
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.729
Here's the picture, from a real simulation: eight tanh layers,

9
00:00:32.729 --> 00:00:35.833
two hundred fifty six units each, the same input.

10
00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:41.767
Weights too small, and each layer shrinks the signal, until every activation piles up at zero.

11
00:00:42.067 --> 00:00:47.667
Weights too big, and tanh saturates, so activations jam against minus one and plus one.

12
00:00:47.967 --> 00:00:51.527
Xavier scaling keeps a healthy spread, layer after layer.

13
00:00:51.527 --> 00:00:54.400
Gradients behave the same way on the way back.

14
00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:59.733
The fix is to choose the initial variance so the factor is about one.

15
00:01:00.033 --> 00:01:04.400
Xavier or Glorot init balances the signal for tanh and sigmoid.

16
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:09.733
He or Kaiming init doubles it, to compensate for ReLU killing half its inputs.

17
00:01:10.933 --> 00:01:12.833
Let's apply it to a deep network.

18
00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:15.300
Ten stacked linear and ReLU blocks.

19
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.333
Each linear layer gets Kaiming normal init, designed for ReLU.

20
00:01:19.633 --> 00:01:21.869
And apply runs it over every layer.

21
00:01:21.869 --> 00:01:25.000
Gradient norms now stay the same size throughout.

22
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:27.367
Here's where it bites.

23
00:01:27.667 --> 00:01:31.833
You port a twelve layer encoder from a paper, swapping tanh for ReLU.

24
00:01:32.133 --> 00:01:33.430
Training flatlines.

25
00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:36.433
The paper's Xavier init was matched to tanh,

26
00:01:36.433 --> 00:01:42.167
and under ReLU the signal decays until layer one's gradients reach one e minus nine.

27
00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:45.033
Switching to He init fixes it on the first try.

28
00:01:46.233 --> 00:01:47.149
One more trap.

29
00:01:47.149 --> 00:01:53.035
All zeros is even worse: every neuron in a layer is identical and can never differentiate.

30
00:01:53.035 --> 00:01:54.867
That's the symmetry problem.

31
00:01:55.167 --> 00:01:58.633
Use random weights, with variance matched to your activation.

32
00:01:59.833 --> 00:02:00.603
Let's recap.

33
00:02:00.603 --> 00:02:04.067
Initialization sets the signal factor for every layer.

34
00:02:04.367 --> 00:02:06.800
Too small vanishes, too large explodes.

35
00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.200
Xavier for tanh, He for ReLU.

36
00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:12.181
Watch for it in custom or ported code.

37
00:02:12.181 --> 00:02:14.933
Next up: convolutional neural networks.
